//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
// The Tier-0 machine: the development Mac, for the zero-cost protocol shakedown
// (vx-review#13) before any cluster is rented. Its job is not to produce data
// about Apple silicon -- it is to run predict -> measure -> compare end to end so
// the harness's own bugs surface here rather than on a metered box.
//
// This SKU is interesting precisely because it is the degenerate case. There is
// no discrete device memory: CPU and GPU address the same physical DRAM, so the
// host<->device "transfer" that dominates every other fleet file is, here, a
// memcpy within one memory. A model that cannot say something sensible about a
// seam whose cost is nearly zero is a model that will mispredict the interesting
// seams too.
//
// It also declares the shared vocabulary (`HBM` / `L2` / `SMEM`) like every other
// fleet file, so the same admission program runs against it unchanged -- which is
// itself part of what the shakedown checks.
//
//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//

// spec: Apple M4 (MacBook Air, T8132), 10-core (4P+6E), 32 GiB unified memory as
// configured on the development machine (`sysctl hw.memsize` = 34359738368).
// UNVERIFIED bandwidth: Apple publishes 120 GB/s for the base M4 (LPDDR5X-7500 on
// a 128-bit bus). That is the *hardware peak* for the memory system as a whole,
// not an achievable single-threaded copy rate, and the gap between the two is
// exactly what experiment M1 measures. Declared as the peak on purpose: the
// shakedown's error table is only informative if the declared figure is the
// vendor's claim rather than a number already fitted to the measurement.
//
// Named `HBM` rather than `UNIFIED` to keep the fleet's shared space vocabulary
// (fleet/README.md): one program text, every SKU, swap the flag.
Memory HBM {
  capacity: 32 GiB, bandwidth: 120 GB/s, managed: explicit, scope: device
}

// spec: Apple M4 P-core cluster L2 = 16 MiB (`sysctl hw.perflevel0.l2cachesize`
// = 16777216, read off the development machine rather than transcribed).
// UNVERIFIED bandwidth: Apple publishes no L2 bandwidth figure. 400 GB/s is a
// placeholder of the right order for a shared cluster L2; M1 measures it, and
// until then no claim rests on it.
Memory L2 {
  within: Memory::HBM, capacity: 16 MiB, bandwidth: 400 GB/s, managed: cached, scope: device
}

// The Apple GPU's threadgroup memory. spec: Apple 9-family threadgroup memory
// limit is 32 KiB per threadgroup. UNVERIFIED bandwidth: declared in B/cyc to
// match how every other fleet file describes on-chip scratchpad, so a program
// that places a tile here is costed in the same units across SKUs.
Memory SMEM {
  within: Memory::L2, capacity: 32 KiB, bandwidth: 128 B/cyc, granule: 1 KiB, managed: explicit, scope: sm
}

// The degenerate seam. On a discrete part this edge is PCIe or C2C and dominates
// the staging cost; here the "transfer" is a memcpy inside one physical memory,
// so its rate is a *copy* rate rather than a link rate.
//
// UNVERIFIED, and deliberately declared as the same 120 GB/s peak: the shakedown
// exists to measure how far that is from the truth. A memcpy both reads and
// writes, so it moves 2 bytes of traffic per byte copied and cannot exceed half
// the peak even in principle -- the error table should show the model
// over-predicting throughput by at least 2x, and if it does not, the harness is
// wrong rather than the hardware surprising. That is the pre-registered
// expectation this dry run is testing.
Topology Device {
  // The Apple GPU. Named, and deliberately not an LLVM target triple: there is
  // no upstream backend for this ISA, and Vx does not emit code for it -- the
  // part is reached through CoreML behind the plugin ABI (runtime/npu_dispatch.mm).
  // So a triple derived from this must be refused rather than guessed at, which is
  // the honest answer for a machine that is dispatched to rather than compiled for.
  arch: applegpu,
  memory: Memory::HBM,
  visible: [Memory::HBM, Memory::L2, Memory::SMEM],
  transfer Memory::CPU_DRAM -> Memory::HBM : 120 GB/s,
  transfer Memory::HBM -> Memory::L2,
  transfer Memory::L2 -> Memory::SMEM
}
